<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marmaduke Grove</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marmaduke Grove was a Chilean air force officer and socialist politician. On 4 June 1932 he led the coup that overthrew President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-esteban-montero">Juan Esteban Montero</a> and proclaimed the Socialist Republic of Chile, which lasted only twelve days before a rival coup exiled him. In 1933 he helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialist-party-of-chile">Socialist Party of Chile</a> and later served as a senator.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marmaduke-grove.jpg" alt="Marmaduke Grove" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cdte._Marmaduke_Grove_.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Grove was born in Copiapó, the son of the lawyer José Marmaduke Grove Avalos and Ana Vallejo Burgos. In 1891 his father, a local leader of the Radical Party, was imprisoned for criticising President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-manuel-balmaceda">José Manuel Balmaceda</a>. Grove entered the Chilean Naval Academy in 1892 but was expelled in 1894 for joining a student revolt against its directors. He entered the Military Academy in 1897, where his classmates included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-ibanez-del-campo">Carlos Ibáñez del Campo</a> and Arturo Puga, and graduated as an artillery sub-lieutenant. He trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1906 to 1911 and had become a Freemason by 1912.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Grove was Under-Director of the Military Academy from 1920 to 1924 and Director of the Air Force Academy from 1925. On 3 September 1924 he took part in the saber rattling, a protest by 56 officers over low pay, and carried the military committee's petitions to the president; the crisis ended with the resignation of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arturo-alessandri">Arturo Alessandri</a>. Sent abroad as military attaché once Ibáñez was in power, Grove conspired with the exiled Alessandri and signed the Calais pact on 17 January 1929. In November that year he was retired from the army and deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>.</p><p>On 21 September 1930 he flew to Concepción in a small red plane to raise a revolt. It failed, and he was deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Easter Island</a>, from which he escaped on a French corvette. He returned after the fall of Ibáñez in 1931, and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-esteban-montero">Juan Esteban Montero</a> made him Air Force Commander-in-chief in March 1932. During the Socialist Republic he was Defense minister from 5 to 16 June 1932. In that year's presidential election, back from a second exile on Easter Island only two days before the vote, he came second to Alessandri with 17.7 per cent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>With Oscar Schnake, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador-allende">Salvador Allende</a> and Carlos Alberto Martínez he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialist-party-of-chile">Socialist Party of Chile</a> on 19 April 1933, became its General Secretary in 1938 and presided over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-chile">Popular Front</a>, which won that year's presidential election with Pedro Aguirre Cerda. Elected senator in a by-election on 9 May 1934 while held in jail, he proposed Chile's first plan of agrarian reform in 1939 under the slogan &quot;Neither land without men nor men without land&quot;. He was re-elected in 1941, left the Senate in 1949 after a defeat by Ibáñez, and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> on 15 May 1954.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marmaduke Grove” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 4 June 1932 the air force commander <b>Marmaduke Grove</b> and other officers, with the politicians Carlos Dávila and Eugenio Matte, overthrew President Juan Esteban Montero and proclaimed the Socialist Republic of Chile.</blockquote><a href="/state/socialist-republic-of-chile">Socialist Republic of Chile</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Copiapó, in what is now <a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-27.37,-70.33&amp;year=1927" aria-label="Copiapó on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2702 3167 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Marmaduke Grove"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3152 3437h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3152 3437h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-27.37,-70.33&amp;year=1927">What was here in 1927? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
